    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Without revealing anything that they have or don't have. I think it's ill-advised. I don't think it's a good idea. But in terms of reading the tea leaves, I think the president is generous in his disposition around speaking to Republicans is willingness to have these conversations. I do not personally think that we should take stock from one comment in one moment. I think it's important to read a pattern. And in more recent comments, I've seen him kind of walking that back. And also, I think it's important to understand here that because Kevin McCarthy does not have the votes in his caucus and not by four or five, but by 20, 30, 40, or potentially 50 votes, the White House needs to understand how many Democrats do they have that would actually vote for these kinds of cuts. You have the Progressive Caucus right now that is about 100 members. And many of them probably automatically off the board. You have the Congressional Black Caucus that just issued a statement very forcefully coming out. And so that brings you now to maybe about 100 to 150 Democrats potentially off the board. And so if they need 50, that really starts to make the democratic ability to opt into a deal. When Kevin McCarthy is so short, it starts making that math difficult. And that is where I believe Hakeem Jeffries plays a role here. Just because Republicans have the majority, what they need is that they need us to bail them out here. And when the tables are flipped, There have been moments where we have had extraordinary democratic unity, but we had five defections, right, from perhaps a more moderate end of the",
