    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95969.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "I do think that there were a couple of moments. Well, the actual passage of the bill, the timing of the bipartisan infrastructure law in and of itself was due to a response to media and due to a response in public narrative. It was a concern that that the narrative around the ability to govern was getting away from us and that is why this needs to happen now and that's why it needs to happen in this fashion. And so I think it is very important that the public is not just reading tea leaves or trying to understand the palace intrigue, but that we have this conversation from the vantage point, not of what do we think is going to happen, but what do we want to happen? How do we make ourselves clear about what is best for people and what is not best for people? And that is when we need to kind of take a step back from having so much of the oxygen in our public conversation being about gamesmanship or negotiation or this and that as opposed to do we want veterans health care to be cut right now. I mean we really need to talk about the actual consequences of this. pandemic, SNAP and pandemic food assistance has expired, and we are seeing explosions in food insecurity across the country. We're seeing it in New York City, but we're seeing in rural areas, suburban areas everywhere. To cut food assistance, potentially in a time after those cuts have already happened, after the COVID phase out, it would be devastating. And when we center and make sure that we take command of public narrative and become voices and shape and help educate the public about what the true stakes of this conversation are, then we can become that fifth person in the room. A president with public opinion has far more leverage than a president without it. And it's very important, I think, that we get in the game and really explain the stakes to the American people.",
