    "turn_idx": 143,
    "turn_text": "I think, of course, substantively digging into what we've been working on in changing approaches to things like housing, disaster response, et cetera. I mean, really our substantive work. But also, I think, in terms of politics, I don't think that there is, in general, We talk about elections in a completely broken way and the political dynamics that determine elections in a totally broken way. I think that very often it's cast in an ideological left to right to right to middle. And there is not enough attention being paid on who is building movements on the ground for longer term infrastructures to win. And that to me is far more determinative of the outcome of an election as opposed to narrative or decisions that are left, right, progressive, moderate, conservative, right-wing, left-wing, there really isn't enough paid attention or questions asked about who is building real power in communities. And if we did pay more attention to that, I think we would do much better and people would be better off understanding what is actually materially impacting them.",
